93ddcaf-e7d3-52fa-b839-ddb952fa312d" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7f52e38a-24e3-5e4b-ac01-1a4c1fa10cf8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f52e38a-24e3-5e4b-ac01-1a4c1fa10cf8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f52e38a-24e3-5e4b-ac01-1a4c1fa10cf8" :answer/id #uuid "7f52e38a-24e3-5e4b-ac01-1a4c1fa10cf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5cd3c5e6-c84b-55d0-9b74-8cf6164e62a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd3c5e6-c84b-55d0-9b74-8cf6164e62a1" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9ac63b60-993d-5f75-a07c-b064fe44237b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ac63b60-993d-5f75-a07c-b064fe44237b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ac63b60-993d-5f75-a07c-b064fe44237b" :answer/id #uuid "9ac63b60-993d-5f75-a07c-b064fe44237b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5a76ab5b-a059-5458-b5f4-e674816ed366" :question/id #uuid "5a76ab5b-a059-5458-b5f4-e674816ed366" :question/math-academy-id "q-253488" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $X=PD{P}^{-1},$ find ${X}^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={x}^{5}$ and the given matrix $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}^{5}=(PD{P}^{-1})^{5}=P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n{D}^{5}=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{2}{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{5} & =P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -96 & 64\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d52e4f2e-ac8e-5bd0-a3c8-e99ceefdc963"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d52e4f2e-ac8e-5bd0-a3c8-e99ceefdc963" :answer-field/id #uuid "d52e4f2e-ac8e-5bd0-a3c8-e99ceefdc963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-086b5f8c-361e-5e84-ba7f-07908a6ffad8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-086b5f8c-361e-5e84-ba7f-07908a6ffad8" "answer-8e0c78fd-212a-50ef-8305-029fa98e0cf5" "answer-774e359d-a732-5eac-9259-4f31c4ad4889" "answer-6915da6f-7547-5e67-bf4a-3730ded795b5" "answer-5f32c9ba-7882-5683-b822-c627858b4d7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-086b5f8c-361e-5e84-ba7f-07908a6ffad8" :answer/id #uuid "086b5f8c-361e-5e84-ba7f-07908a6ffad8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e0c78fd-212a-50ef-8305-029fa98e0cf5" :answer/id #uuid "8e0c78fd-212a-50ef-8305-029fa98e0cf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}31 & 46 \\\\ -67 & 98\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-774e359d-a732-5eac-9259-4f31c4ad4889" :answer/id #uuid "774e359d-a732-5eac-9259-4f31c4ad4889" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}32 & 24 \\\\ -18 & 180\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6915da6f-7547-5e67-bf4a-3730ded795b5" :answer/id #uuid "6915da6f-7547-5e67-bf4a-3730ded795b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}62 & 92 \\\\ -125 & 186\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f32c9ba-7882-5683-b822-c627858b4d7d" :answer/id #uuid "5f32c9ba-7882-5683-b822-c627858b4d7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}72 & -52 \\\\ -166 & 75\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157270 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7819c8fe-793f-5300-ba05-a00d3d72cc5c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7819c8fe-793f-5300-ba05-a00d3d72cc5c" :question/id #uuid "7819c8fe-793f-5300-ba05-a00d3d72cc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-253884" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{1}(3) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(0)\\end{aligned}])} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(0),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(0)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(0)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(0)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0} & {e}^{0} & \\frac{{e}^{0}}{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{0} & {e}^{0} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-506c651f-f5b6-52a6-9177-275eb76f9e87" "answer-field-9fabe0ba-2f06-5d33-ae09-be09b22d98ca" "answer-field-e436c140-e683-55ba-81fe-1b3bbe91bf3f" "answer-field-25dde8d8-265c-54c9-ac3f-e40a69e95898" "answer-field-e5c61432-7357-5400-9143-63f15ef64c3f" "answer-field-d4498fa0-0077-5245-b464-34ae73a87b7d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-506c651f-f5b6-52a6-9177-275eb76f9e87" :answer-field/id #uuid "506c651f-f5b6-52a6-9177-275eb76f9e87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4c37bfdb-2dfe-5cc2-840b-243db54502a0" :